export const articles = [
{
slug:'remodeling-condos', title:'Condo Remodeling in San Diego: HOA, Permits & Shared Systems',
description:'Plan a San Diego condo remodel around HOA approval, permits, shared plumbing, flooring rules, delivery access and building work hours.',
intro:'A condo remodel involves more than the space inside your front door. Before pricing a new kitchen or bathroom, find out which parts of the building you can alter, who approves the work and how the crew will get materials in. Those answers can change the layout and the construction schedule.',
sections:[
['Get the association’s remodeling packet first',`<p>Ask the manager for the current architectural application, construction rules and governing documents. The California Department of Real Estate explains how CC&Rs and association rules govern common-interest developments. Use those documents to establish responsibilities; a neighbor’s past remodel is not approval for yours. ${ref('hoa')}.</p><p>Request written answers about flooring assemblies, waterproofing submissions, contractor insurance documentation, work hours, deposits, elevator reservations and water shutoffs. Some buildings impose detailed requirements; others do not. The estimate should reflect your building’s actual rules rather than a standard condo surcharge.</p><p>For a detached home in an HOA, the same first step applies, especially for exterior work. Shared elevators and vertical plumbing stacks may be irrelevant, but association review and municipal approval still need separate attention.</p>`],
['Keep association approval and the permit separate',`<p>A board may approve a finish or layout while the building department still needs plans. Conversely, a permit does not settle an association’s ownership or architectural restrictions. For property inside the City of San Diego, start with its ${ref('building')}; other cities have their own departments.</p><p>Submit a consistent scope to both reviewers. If the approved association drawing retains the sink but the permit drawing moves it, resolve the difference before ordering cabinets. Keep the approval conditions with the contractor’s working set.</p>`],
['Check what sits behind the wall and below the floor','<p>A proposed sink move may interact with a shared drain or a ceiling in the unit below. Before committing to it, identify the pipe route, access permissions and restoration responsibility. Ask whether structural or fire-resistance details limit penetrations. Do not assume that a wall belongs entirely to you because its painted surface is inside the unit.</p><p>Flooring deserves its own review. Ask the association for the required sound-control documentation and accepted assembly, including the underlayment and finish. A product advertised as quiet is not proof that the installed floor meets the building’s rules.</p>'],
['Price the route to the room','<p>Walk the delivery route with the contractor: loading space, elevator dimensions, door openings, protected corridors and the final turn into the unit. Confirm where debris can wait and when it must leave. A large countertop may need a different seam arrangement if it cannot reach the kitchen as drawn.</p><p>Put shutoff notice periods, reserved delivery days and limited work hours into the schedule. These are practical constraints to price before work begins, not reasons to discover extra charges halfway through.</p>'],
['Before you authorize construction','<ul><li>Collect the written association decision and any conditions.</li><li>Confirm municipal permits and the responsible applicant.</li><li>Approve plumbing routes, flooring assembly and access arrangements.</li><li>Identify who coordinates building notices and who repairs common-area damage.</li><li>Agree on a fallback layout if a shared-system alteration is not allowed.</li></ul>']
],faq:[['Can I start while the board is reviewing the application?','Wait for the approvals required for your scope. Have the manager identify whether any limited work can proceed separately, and confirm the permit status before scheduling that work.'],['Will keeping the existing layout simplify the project?','Often it reduces the number of shared-system questions, but flooring, waterproofing, electrical work and building access can still need review. Price the actual work rather than assuming that an unchanged floor plan makes it permit-free.']],sources:['hoa','building'],links:[['Bathroom permit planning','../bathroom-remodel-permit-california.html'],['Kitchen layout decisions','kitchen-layout-design.html'],['Request a condo project consultation','../contact.html']]
},
{
slug:'unpermitted-work',title:'Unpermitted Work in a San Diego Home: What to Check First',
description:'Find the records, define uncertain work and discuss a correction path before remodeling a San Diego home with potentially unpermitted alterations.',
intro:'If an old addition or converted garage has uncertain permit history, investigate before building your new remodel around it. The first task is to establish what was approved and what exists today. Missing paperwork alone does not prove that the work was illegal, and a permit number alone does not prove that it received final approval.',
sections:[
['Build a record of the existing space',`<p>Collect the address, parcel information, available permit numbers, old drawings, inspection records and dated photographs. Ask the responsible city or County department what records it holds. The City of San Diego provides a ${ref('records')}. A property in an incorporated city is not automatically handled by the County simply because it is in San Diego County.</p><p>Have the space measured and compare it with the records. Mark differences such as an added bathroom, enclosed patio, altered garage door or moved wall. Keep uncertain items visible in the project notes instead of presenting them as approved existing work.</p>`],
['Ask what correction route applies to this particular work',`<p>Describe the condition to the permitting authority before choosing a repair strategy. A garage used as a separate dwelling raises different questions from a relocated sink. In the City of San Diego, ${ref('legalize')} describes a specific process for qualifying unpermitted ADUs. It is not blanket permission to keep every unpermitted addition or conversion.</p><p>Ask which drawings, evidence of construction date, inspections and corrections are needed. If there is an enforcement notice, give the project team the complete notice and case number. Do not treat a new application as a reason to disregard its deadlines or directions.</p>`],
['Leave room in the scope for investigation','<p>An estimate based only on visible finishes cannot confirm hidden foundations, wiring or drainage. Identify what the designer or inspector needs to see and how openings will be made and repaired. Separate the investigation from the final corrective work so the first authorization does not pretend to settle unknown costs.</p><p>For example, a planned cabinet installation may depend on keeping a wall that was moved years ago. Confirming that wall’s role and approval history before fabricating cabinets is more useful than adding a vague “unforeseen conditions” line to the proposal.</p>'],
['Compare correction with redesign','<p>Once requirements are known, compare retaining and correcting the space with revising the remodel to avoid relying on it. Include drawings, engineering, access, restoration and the effect on the rest of the house. There is no reliable universal fee or percentage that makes all old work legal.</p><p>ADELIE can discuss construction scope and sequencing. Questions about a purchase disclosure, insurance coverage or a dispute with a seller belong with the appropriate real-estate, insurance or legal adviser.</p>'],
['Keep the final record, not just the application','<p>At completion, retain the approved plans, corrections and final inspection status for the authorized work. A receipt for an application documents submission; it does not establish that construction was accepted. Keep a clear list of anything outside the corrected scope so a future project does not repeat the same uncertainty.</p>']
],faq:[['Can I just apply for the permit after the remodel?','That can leave you needing to expose or change finished work. Establish the required process before continuing the affected construction. The authority determines what can be approved and how it must be verified.'],['Does every unpermitted room have to be demolished?','There is no single answer. The correction options depend on the work, location and applicable requirements. Do not accept a promise that every condition can be legalized, or assume removal is necessary before the property has been evaluated.']],sources:['records','legalize'],links:[['Planning work in older homes','older-san-diego-homes.html'],['How permit review and inspections connect','plan-review.html'],['Project Rescue','../project-rescue.html']]
},
{
slug:'older-san-diego-homes',title:'Remodeling an Older San Diego Home: Investigate Before Pricing',
description:'Plan an older-home remodel around permit records, historic review, lead-safe preparation, existing systems and the details worth preserving.',
intro:'An older home can have several generations of work hidden behind one finished wall. The useful question is not simply how old the house is. It is which parts of the existing construction your new design will rely on, and what needs to be verified before you commit to that design.',
sections:[
['Start with a measured plan and a short uncertainty list','<p>Gather original drawings, later permit records and photos of previous renovations. Compare them with the rooms as they exist. Record uneven floors, recurring stains, inaccessible crawl spaces and unexplained changes in ceiling height without jumping to a diagnosis.</p><p>Ask the project team to separate known work from investigation: for example, replacing a visible fixture is a defined task; deciding whether an old concealed pipe can remain may need access and evaluation. That distinction makes estimates easier to compare.</p>'],
['Check historic review before changing exterior details',`<p>For property in the City of San Diego, age can affect the review route even when a house has no historic plaque. The City’s guidance addresses work on buildings 45 years old or older, with scope-dependent review and exceptions. Check the parcel and proposal with staff before replacing windows or removing original exterior features. ${ref('historic')}.</p><p>Other local cities have their own processes. Preserve photographs and dimensions of trim, doors, windows and roof details you hope to retain. Ask whether repair, replication or replacement fits both the project and the applicable review.</p>`],
['Plan investigation without creating an exposure problem',`<p>EPA provides specific lead-safe renovation guidance for pre-1978 housing. Before disturbing painted surfaces in a home of that age, discuss the applicable assessment and work practices with a qualified renovation professional. ${ref('lead')}.</p><p>EPA also cautions against disturbing suspect asbestos-containing materials without appropriate professional evaluation. Do not use a casual demolition opening to test what a material is. Include any needed evaluation and controlled work in the plan before ordinary demolition starts. ${ref('air')}.</p>`],
['Coordinate the systems you will uncover','<p>Use the proposed layout to ask focused questions: can the electrical system support the selected appliances, where will a relocated drain run, and can heating or cooling reach the altered room? Have the relevant professionals evaluate the systems rather than assuming that every older component must be replaced—or that every working component can stay.</p><p>Schedule agreed investigations before long-lead orders where possible. If a new beam or duct route will lower the ceiling, the cabinet design needs that information before fabrication. Show restoration of opened walls and adjoining finishes in the estimate.</p>'],
['Preserve character with a room-by-room decision list','<p>Label existing features “retain,” “repair,” “match” or “replace.” Include photos of details that matter to you, such as a built-in cabinet or the transition between original flooring and an addition. A clear list prevents salvageable material from disappearing during demolition.</p><p>For the budget, keep a reserve tied to unresolved conditions and identify when each unknown should become a priced decision. Use the findings to adjust scope before spending the reserve on finish upgrades.</p>']
],faq:[['Does an older home automatically need a complete rewire?','Age alone cannot answer that. Have the electrical system assessed against its condition, proposed loads and the work being permitted. The estimate should explain the basis for keeping or replacing components.'],['Can I keep original windows and still remodel the interior?','Possibly. The interior scope, condition of the windows and applicable historic or energy requirements matter. Show which windows remain and which change so the designer and reviewing authority can evaluate the proposal accurately.']],sources:['historic','lead','air'],links:[['Investigating uncertain permit history','unpermitted-work.html'],['Budget allowances and reserves','allowances.html'],['Whole-home remodeling services','../whole-home-remodeling.html']]
},
{
slug:'adu-feasibility',title:'San Diego ADU Feasibility: Site, Utilities & Access',
description:'Evaluate a San Diego ADU before committing to plans: jurisdiction, usable site area, utility routes, equipment access and a complete project scope.',
intro:'A floor plan that fits in the backyard is only the beginning of ADU feasibility. Before choosing finishes, establish where the unit can go, how it will connect to services and how it can be built without overlooking the existing home. Those checks help distinguish a promising concept from a complete project budget.',
sections:[
['Confirm the authority and the type of unit',`<p>Start with the parcel address and identify the responsible city or County department. For the City of San Diego, ${ref('adu')} covers ADUs, JADUs and permit requirements, and warns that coastal rules differ. Ask staff to classify your proposed detached unit, attached addition or conversion before relying on a generic ADU checklist.</p><p>Record the intended use and occupants, the existing dwelling configuration and any known easements. Avoid assuming that a preapproved floor plan establishes approval for the site; location, utility and site-specific documents still need attention.</p>`],
['Draw the site beyond the new walls','<p>A useful concept plan shows the existing house, proposed unit, property boundaries, access route, major trees, slopes and known utilities. Add drainage direction and the outdoor space you want to keep. Ask the designer which boundaries or elevations need a survey before the layout becomes detailed.</p><p>Compare two placements if the lot permits it. A slightly different orientation might improve privacy or shorten a utility route. Evaluate that tradeoff alongside bedroom size rather than choosing a layout solely by its advertised square footage.</p>'],
['Establish a utility plan early',`<p>Ask a qualified electrician or plumber to identify the likely connection points and the information needed for service planning. Do not assume the existing panel has spare capacity or that a sewer line follows the shortest visible path. List what has been verified and what requires locating, calculations or further investigation.</p><p>SDG&E asks applicants to gather project documentation and appliance-load information. Its service application guidance also says an ADU address must be established through the municipality before applying. Confirm the current requirements directly with the utility. ${ref('power')}.</p><p>Keep utility-provider work, private trenching, equipment and pavement restoration as separate estimate items. That makes exclusions visible without pretending the utility’s final scope is already known.</p>`],
['Walk the equipment and delivery route','<p>Measure access at gates, corners and overhead obstructions. Identify where excavated soil, materials and debris can wait. If equipment cannot use the proposed route, ask how the work will be done and how the pricing changes.</p><p>Consider daily access to the existing home, pets, laundry and parking during construction. Put fence removal and replacement, landscape repairs and temporary protection into the scope. These tasks are easy to miss when the estimate starts and ends at the new building’s walls.</p>'],
['Make a feasibility decision before a finish decision','<ul><li>Document the jurisdiction’s response to the proposed unit and location.</li><li>Identify surveys, design work and investigations still needed.</li><li>List utility assumptions and the next person responsible for resolving each one.</li><li>Compare the building price with a total that includes site work, fees and restoration.</li><li>Choose the next design stage only after those costs and uncertainties fit your budget.</li></ul>']
],faq:[['Does a small ADU automatically have a simple utility connection?','No. Size is only one input. Existing service capacity, connection locations and the site route can matter as much as the unit’s floor area.'],['Should I choose a detached ADU or convert the garage?','Compare both against the property. A garage offers an existing enclosure, but its slab, structure and conversion requirements may change the economics. A detached unit can offer different privacy and layout options while adding new site work.']],sources:['adu','power'],links:[['Garage conversion feasibility','garage-conversion.html'],['San Diego ADU costs','../adu-cost-san-diego.html'],['ADU construction services','../adu-construction.html']]
},
{
slug:'garage-conversion',title:'Garage Conversion in San Diego: What Must Be Checked?',
description:'Compare a garage conversion with other ADU options by checking the slab, structure, utilities, approvals and the work hidden behind a simple floor plan.',
intro:'Keeping the garage walls does not necessarily make a conversion inexpensive. The important question is how much of the existing building can serve the proposed living space. Evaluate that before treating the garage’s square footage as ready-made housing.',
sections:[
['Decide what the converted space will be',`<p>A room attached to the main home, an independent ADU and a JADU are different proposals. Describe the kitchen, bathroom, access and intended arrangement to the local department. Inside the City of San Diego, use ${ref('adu')} to identify the relevant application path. Parking and coastal provisions need a location-specific check; do not promise that every conversion has the same parking exemption.</p><p>Check the existing garage’s records and actual dimensions. If it has already been altered, establish which parts were approved before designing around them.</p>`],
['Investigate the floor before drawing the bathroom','<p>Have the design team evaluate slab condition, levels and the proposed finish height. Work out how the bathroom drain will connect and what excavation or other construction the route requires. A neat bathroom arrangement on a plan can become difficult if it ignores the existing floor or connection depth.</p><p>Include moisture evaluation and a floor-system specification appropriate to the conditions. Covering an uncertain slab with a new finish is not the same as resolving its suitability for occupied space.</p>'],
['Evaluate the enclosure as a whole','<p>Ask which existing walls, roof members and foundations can remain and what verification supports that decision. Coordinate insulation, windows, doors, weather protection and heating or cooling with the proposed use. Replacing the garage door with a wall is one part of that work, not a complete conversion scope.</p><p>A retained roof or wall can save work, but repairing around it may limit the layout. Compare retaining it with rebuilding the affected portion before assuming reuse is always the cheaper option.</p>'],
['Put services and the surrounding property in the estimate','<p>Locate the proposed electrical, water and wastewater connections. Identify whether separate utility coordination is needed and which costs remain provisional. Include trenching, driveway repairs and any relocated storage or equipment.</p><p>Think through what the garage currently does. If it contains laundry, a water heater, household storage or the main access route, decide where those functions will go. The replacement arrangements are part of the project even though they may sit outside the new dwelling.</p>'],
['Compare like-for-like options','<p>Ask for a scope comparison between conversion and a new detached unit with similar usable rooms and finishes. List retained construction, corrective work, site work and outstanding investigations separately. A conversion quote that excludes service work cannot be compared directly with a detached-unit quote that includes it.</p><p>There is no responsible universal conversion discount. Use the property findings to decide whether the existing garage is an advantage, a constraint or a mixture of both.</p>']
],faq:[['Can I move into the converted garage once the finishes are done?','Confirm the required final approvals with the permitting authority before using it as living space. Completed flooring and appliances do not establish that the permitted work has been accepted.'],['Will a conversion always be faster than new construction?','Not necessarily. Existing conditions, corrections and utility coordination can affect either option. Ask for a sequence tied to the actual approvals and investigations.']],sources:['adu','inspections'],links:[['Full ADU feasibility checklist','adu-feasibility.html'],['Unpermitted work and existing conversions','unpermitted-work.html'],['ADU project budgets','../adu-cost-san-diego.html']]
},
{
slug:'structural-changes',title:'Removing a Wall During a Remodel: Structure, Permits & Repairs',
description:'Understand what to establish before removing a wall: structural design, support below, utilities, inspections and the surrounding finish repairs.',
intro:'Before removing a wall, find out what it supports and what runs through it. A wall’s appearance cannot establish whether it is safe to remove. The construction scope may include a beam, posts, work below the floor and relocated services—not just demolition and patching.',
sections:[
['Have the existing structure evaluated','<p>Bring the proposed opening, available drawings and access information to the designer or structural engineer. The investigation should establish how loads move through the building, including where proposed supports would bear. A ceiling view or a neighbor’s similar floor plan is not a substitute for evaluating this house.</p><p>Ask what must be opened or measured to complete the design. Price that investigation separately if necessary. Avoid selecting a beam or temporary support arrangement from a rule of thumb; those decisions depend on the actual structure.</p>'],
['Design the connection, not just the opening','<p>A wider opening can affect the space above, the supports at each end and the foundation below. Review proposed post locations on the furniture and cabinet plan. A support that works structurally may still obstruct a doorway or leave less usable counter space than expected.</p><p>Compare the preferred clear opening with an option retaining a short wall or accepting a visible beam. A smaller change may meet the household’s circulation goal with less surrounding work. That is a design comparison, not an assurance that either option avoids a permit.</p>'],
['Coordinate the services and finish boundaries','<p>Identify wiring, switches, outlets, supply piping, drains and ducts that may use the wall. Assign each relocation to the appropriate trade and show the revised locations before rough work begins. Decide how continuous flooring, ceilings and adjacent cabinets will be restored.</p><p>Ask the estimate to name the repair limits. “Patch wall” does not tell you whether the ceiling texture, painting to a natural break or missing flooring beneath the old wall is included.</p>'],
['Plan approvals and the inspection sequence',`<p>Discuss the structural proposal with the responsible building department. The City of San Diego’s ${ref('building')} explains its construction-permit process. Have the contractor coordinate the engineered construction sequence, temporary support and access with the approved documents before demolition.</p><p>Some designs require special inspections or structural observations. These are not interchangeable with the regular building inspections. San Diego explains the distinction in its ${ref('inspections')}. Confirm who arranges each required visit and what must remain visible before work is covered.</p>`],
['Questions to settle before demolition','<ul><li>What investigation supports the design?</li><li>Where do new supports land, including below the floor?</li><li>Who is responsible for temporary support and trade coordination?</li><li>Which inspections or observations are required?</li><li>What flooring, ceiling, paint and cabinet repairs are included?</li></ul>']
],faq:[['Can a non-load-bearing wall still be expensive to remove?','Yes. Utility relocation and restoration can be substantial even when structural support is not the main issue. Confirm the wall’s role professionally, then price the complete surrounding work.'],['Does a bigger beam eliminate the need for posts or foundation work?','That cannot be assumed. The engineer evaluates the entire load path and connections. Increasing one component does not establish that the rest of the structure is adequate.']],sources:['building','inspections'],links:[['Older-home investigations','older-san-diego-homes.html'],['Permits through final inspection','plan-review.html'],['Home additions','../home-additions.html']]
},
{
slug:'shower-waterproofing',title:'Shower Waterproofing: What to Verify Before Tile Goes In',
description:'Ask the right questions about shower waterproofing, drain connections, benches, niches and water testing before the tile covers the work.',
intro:'The waterproofing decision happens before you choose the final grout color. A shower needs a planned system that handles water at the floor, walls, corners and drain. Ask to see that system in the scope and verify it while the work is still visible.',
sections:[
['Tile is the finish, not the waterproofing plan',`<p>Schluter’s technical guidance explains that tile and grout are not inherently waterproof. The assembly behind them needs a suitable moisture-management system. A description such as “tile shower with sealed grout” leaves the essential construction unspecified. ${ref('waterproof')}.</p><p>Ask the installer to identify the selected assembly and its installation instructions. The scope should name the membrane or liner, substrate, drain connection and treatment of seams and penetrations. A brand name by itself does not explain how those components meet.</p>`],
['Review the awkward details before they are covered','<p>Walk through the proposed bench, niche, curb, doorway and valve openings with the installer. Ask how each is incorporated into the waterproofing assembly and where water drains. Resolve the glass enclosure’s attachment details before someone drills through a finished vulnerable surface.</p><p>For a curbless shower, coordinate the floor buildup, drainage and bathroom transition early. Removing a curb from a standard drawing does not establish a workable barrier-free assembly. Likewise, a steam shower needs a design appropriate to steam exposure rather than an assumption that ordinary shower details are sufficient.</p>'],
['Schedule the water test as a construction step',`<p>Schluter describes water testing as a check before setting tile and gives system-specific waiting and test instructions. Those instructions are an example for its products, not a universal rule for every waterproofing assembly. Have the installer identify the applicable manufacturer instructions and the local inspection requirements. ${ref('test')}.</p><p>Include the test in the schedule and keep the result with the project record. If there is a leak or a failed inspection, correct and verify the affected work before covering it. A photograph of a membrane is useful documentation, but it cannot replace the required test or inspection.</p>`],
['Compare proposals at the assembly level','<p>Ask each bidder to describe substrate preparation, waterproofing, drain work, tile setting, sealant joints and any required inspections. Clarify who addresses damaged framing or an unsuitable existing base found after demolition. This is more revealing than comparing tile square-foot prices alone.</p><p>In an upstairs condo, include the association’s submission requirements and access arrangements. A repair can affect the unit below, so establish the communication and restoration process before work starts.</p>'],
['Keep records that help with future maintenance','<ul><li>Product names and the installation instructions used.</li><li>Photos of the prepared substrate and completed waterproofing details.</li><li>Test and inspection records.</li><li>Approved glass attachment details and fixture locations.</li><li>Care instructions, warranty terms and the contact for a suspected leak.</li></ul><p>After use begins, report persistent dampness, staining or unexplained water loss promptly. Replacing grout is not a diagnosis of a hidden waterproofing problem.</p>']
],faq:[['Can sealing the grout fix a leaking shower?','Do not assume it can. Identify the leak source and the condition of the assembly first. A surface treatment does not establish that the hidden waterproofing and drain connection are sound.'],['Should the shower be tested before or after tile?','The cited manufacturer procedure places its waterproofing test before tile. The installer should confirm the selected system’s sequence and required inspection so problems can be addressed while the work remains accessible.']],sources:['waterproof','test'],links:[['Accessible and walk-in shower planning','accessible-bathroom-design.html'],['Residential steam showers','residential-steam-showers.html'],['Bathroom remodeling services','../bathroom-remodeling.html']]
},
{
slug:'accessible-bathroom-design',title:'Accessible Bathroom & Walk-In Shower Planning for Your Home',
description:'Plan a bathroom around the person using it: entry, movement, shower access, controls, support, lighting and future adaptability.',
intro:'A walk-in shower is not automatically an accessible shower. The right layout depends on how someone enters, turns, reaches the controls and gets assistance if needed. Start with those tasks before choosing a curbless detail or buying fixtures.',
sections:[
['Design for the person and the route into the room','<p>Discuss current and anticipated needs with the household and, where useful, an occupational therapist or other appropriate adviser. Bring the dimensions of any mobility equipment. Consider the approach from the bedroom, the bathroom doorway, maneuvering space and the position of doors and drawers.</p><p>A larger shower can make the rest of a small bathroom harder to use. Draw the whole room, including the toilet and vanity, then test the sequence of getting in, closing the door, using fixtures and leaving. A taped layout can expose conflicts that a product photograph will not.</p>'],
['Use accessibility guidance with the right scope',`<p>The ${ref('access')} illustrates relationships among clearances, seats, grab bars and shower controls. It addresses ADA-covered facilities; it is not evidence that every private-home remodel must follow every ADA dimension. Have the designer identify which local requirements apply and which additional features suit the household.</p><p>A label such as “ADA-style” on a product does not establish compliance of the room. Installation location, surrounding space and the complete arrangement matter.</p>`],
['Coordinate the shower entry with the floor construction','<p>For a curbless or low-threshold entry, establish the available floor depth, structural constraints, drain location and water-containment approach. Work out the shower slope and the transition to the rest of the bathroom before choosing large floor tiles or moving framing.</p><p>Also plan where spray goes when a door is open, whether a seat is needed and how the enclosure affects assistance. The glass layout should support daily use rather than forcing the user to reach around a fixed panel to turn on the water.</p>'],
['Place support and controls before closing the walls','<p>Discuss grab-bar locations and suitable backing while the walls are accessible. Coordinate those locations with valves, wiring, niches and the glass enclosure. Keep dimensioned photos of reinforcement for future work; do not rely on memory after the tile is installed.</p><p>Test the reach to controls from the intended standing or seated position. Consider a handheld shower, reachable storage, lighting at the entry and switches that are easy to operate. Select floor materials for their intended wet use and cleaning needs; no finish eliminates the need to manage water on the floor.</p>'],
['Decide what to build now and what to prepare for','<p>Some features need to be installed now to make the room usable. Others, such as planned reinforcement or an adaptable storage arrangement, may support later changes. Record those choices in the drawings and budget so future adaptability is intentional.</p><p>Before approving the layout, walk through it with the people who will use or help maintain it. Their feedback on reach, transfers, privacy and cleaning is more useful than a generic accessibility checklist alone.</p>']
],faq:[['Does every accessible bathroom need a curbless shower?','No single feature fits every household. Entry height, available space, transfer needs and construction constraints should be evaluated together. A curbless entry can help in some situations but does not make an otherwise cramped room accessible.'],['Can grab bars be added later?','Often, but their anchorage and location need to suit the wall and user. Planning reinforcement during a remodel can make later work easier. Have a qualified installer verify the actual construction before installation.']],sources:['access'],links:[['Shower waterproofing details','shower-waterproofing.html'],['Bathroom planning guide','../bathroom-planning-guide.html'],['Bathroom remodeling consultation','../contact.html']]
},
{
slug:'kitchen-layout-design',title:'Kitchen Layout Planning: Islands, Appliances & Utility Moves',
description:'Test a kitchen layout before ordering cabinets: circulation, island seating, appliance clearances, plumbing routes and electrical planning.',
intro:'A kitchen layout should work when the refrigerator, dishwasher and drawers are open—not just when everything is closed for a rendering. Before ordering cabinets, test the room against the way your household cooks, cleans, stores food and passes through.',
sections:[
['Map the daily conflicts first','<p>List three specific problems with the current kitchen: perhaps two people cannot pass at the sink, the dishwasher blocks the trash pullout, or grocery storage is scattered. Draw the room with door swings, windows and the route to adjoining spaces. Use that drawing to evaluate solutions instead of starting with a preferred island size.</p><p>Mark a proposed island on the floor and simulate occupied seating and open appliances. A walkway that looks generous when empty may be the busiest route to the backyard. Compare an island with a peninsula or a better perimeter layout if the room is tight.</p>'],
['Choose appliance models before releasing cabinet drawings','<p>Obtain the current installation sheets for the actual models, including cutout dimensions, ventilation provisions and service access. “Standard refrigerator” is not a complete specification. Door swing, handle projection and the room needed to remove drawers can affect a nearby wall or island.</p><p>Check the dishwasher door against opposite drawers and passage space. Coordinate sink, faucet and disposal choices with the sink cabinet. If a model changes after approval, have the cabinet and trade drawings checked again before treating it as a harmless substitution.</p>'],
['Treat the island as a small construction project','<p>An island can contain storage, seating, appliances, a sink and electrical service. Each function changes what must fit below the counter and where people stand. Resolve support for the selected counter, end-panel thickness, seating arrangements and appliance access before fabrication.</p><p>If the island contains a sink, ask how drainage and venting will be arranged. If it contains cooking equipment, establish the exhaust route and equipment requirements. Do not make the finished island footprint the only drawing the trades receive.</p>'],
['Compare keeping utilities with moving them',`<p>Ask for a layout retaining the main utility locations alongside the preferred relocated version. Identify the work each option needs in the floor, walls and adjoining finishes. The additional cost may be worthwhile, but it should buy a clear improvement in daily use.</p><p>For a City of San Diego project, ${ref('noplan')} describes eligibility conditions for certain remodel scopes. It is not a general permit exemption. Have the project team confirm the application route for the actual wall, plumbing and electrical changes. Other local jurisdictions use their own procedures.</p>`],
['Approve one coordinated package','<ul><li>Cabinet dimensions, fillers and finished end panels.</li><li>Appliance model numbers and installation sheets.</li><li>Sink, faucet, drain and shutoff locations.</li><li>Lighting, controls and electrical locations reviewed by the responsible professional.</li><li>Countertop dimensions, seams, support and delivery route.</li><li>A decision deadline and change process before fabrication.</li></ul><p>Keep one current drawing set with a revision date. When an appliance, opening or cabinet changes, identify every affected sheet and supplier rather than relying on a verbal update.</p>']
],faq:[['Is adding an island always an improvement?','No. It can reduce circulation or create collisions between seating and appliances. Judge the layout by usable storage and comfortable movement with the kitchen in use.'],['Can I order cabinets before the appliance selection is final?','That introduces avoidable risk. If you must proceed, have the cabinet supplier document exactly which dimensions and assumptions are being accepted, and who bears the cost if the final appliance does not fit.']],sources:['noplan'],links:[['Panel-ready appliance coordination','panel-ready-appliances.html'],['Kitchen costs in San Diego','../kitchen-remodel-cost-san-diego.html'],['Kitchen remodeling services','../kitchen-remodeling.html']]
},
{
slug:'plan-review',title:'San Diego Remodel Permits: From Application to Final Inspection',
description:'Follow a remodel permit through drawings, review comments, issuance, inspections, revisions and final records without confusing one stage with another.',
intro:'Submitting plans, receiving a permit and passing final inspection are different milestones. A workable remodel schedule makes room for all three. Know who is responsible for each step and keep the approved documents connected to the work happening on site.',
sections:[
['Identify the authority and assemble the application',`<p>Confirm whether the address is handled by the City of San Diego, another city or the County. Then ask which application fits the whole scope, including structural changes and related trades. The ${ref('building')} outlines the City’s process and points applicants toward its submittal requirements.</p><p>Organize existing and proposed plans, relevant details, calculations and product information requested for the project. Do not guess a universal document checklist: a kitchen alteration and a new addition can require different material. Assign a person to coordinate the package so the floor plan and trade drawings agree.</p>`],
['Track review comments separately from construction changes',`<p>Keep the reviewers’ comments with the revised drawings and the response identifying how each item was addressed. Confirm that the team is using the latest submitted set. The City’s ${ref('process')} helps distinguish review, issuance and inspection statuses.</p><p>An application accepted for review is not an issued permit. Before scheduling permit-dependent work, verify the actual authorization. Set tentative construction dates around unresolved review items rather than treating a hoped-for approval date as a commitment.</p>`],
['Build inspection pauses into the schedule',`<p>Ask the contractor to identify the inspections required by the approved scope and when work must remain exposed. San Diego’s ${ref('inspections')} describes underground, rough, insulation and final stages, among others. Not every project uses every inspection type.</p><p>Plan time to address corrections before the next trade covers the work. Keep inspection outcomes and correction notices in the project folder. If special inspections are required, confirm the separate arrangements; they do not replace the ordinary building inspections.</p>`],
['Keep field changes connected to the permit','<p>If a proposed change affects the approved work, ask the designer and department how it must be documented and approved. The homeowner’s signed change order and the permit revision serve different purposes: one records the construction agreement, while the other addresses the approved design.</p><p>For example, moving an opening after framing may affect structure, electrical work and exterior details. Resolve the required review before assuming that a marked-up contractor sketch is enough for the final record.</p>'],
['Close the permit as well as the punch list','<p>A cosmetic punch list and the authority’s final approval are separate records. Before calling the project complete, ask for the final inspection status, any outstanding conditions and the current approved drawings. Confirm whether a certificate of occupancy or other additional closeout document applies to the project.</p><p>Save the permit number and records with the home’s maintenance documents. A paid application fee, an issued permit or an invoice marked “final” is not a substitute for evidence of the required final approval.</p>']
],faq:[['Does every remodel need the same inspection sequence?','No. The required stages follow the permitted scope and applicable requirements. Have the contractor confirm the sequence for your project before work begins.'],['Can a project be physically finished while its permit remains open?','Yes. Outstanding inspections or corrections can remain even after finishes are installed. Track the permit status separately from construction progress and resolve open items with the responsible authority.']],sources:['building','process','inspections'],links:[['Which remodel work may need permits','../san-diego-remodel-permit-guide.html'],['Unpermitted existing work','unpermitted-work.html'],['Project closeout checklist','whole-home-closeout.html']]
},
{
slug:'allowances',title:'Remodel Allowances, Contingency & Budget Tracking Explained',
description:'Separate allowances from contingency, account for owner purchases and project fees, and track the cost of selections before approving changes.',
intro:'An allowance is money assigned to something not fully selected or priced. A contingency is a reserve for uncertainty. Neither makes a vague proposal complete. Before comparing remodel bids, establish what each amount covers and how it changes when the real cost is known.',
sections:[
['Ask what is inside each allowance','<p>A tile allowance might cover only the tile purchase, or it might include other items. Ask whether tax, delivery, waste, installation materials and labor are inside the amount. Clarify how credits, overages and any agreed markup are calculated.</p><p>Illustrative example: a contract carries a $3,000 material allowance. The selected material costs $3,600 on the same agreed basis. The material difference is $600; it is not automatically the full change if freight, tax or installation also differs. This is an arithmetic example, not an ADELIE price or a recommended allowance.</p><p>Use actual selections or supplier quotes to test whether an allowance is realistic before signing. A low placeholder can make one bid look cheaper while leaving the same final purchase unresolved.</p>'],
['Keep the contingency tied to identified risks','<p>List unresolved conditions such as concealed piping, uncertain floor preparation or a utility connection awaiting investigation. Set a reserve with the project team based on those risks and the stage of design. There is no single percentage that makes every remodel adequately funded.</p><p>Track the reserve separately from the construction contract. As an unknown becomes defined, record the decision and revised forecast. Do not treat unused reserve as finish-upgrade money while significant investigations remain open.</p>'],
['Count the expenses outside the construction price','<p>Check design, engineering, permits, testing, association charges, temporary accommodation and owner-purchased products. Decide who pays each item and whether it appears elsewhere in the budget. Avoid adding it twice simply because both the contractor and homeowner have mentioned it.</p><p>For each quote, label exclusions explicitly. “Owner supplies appliances” should prompt questions about delivery, storage, installation, connections and damage responsibility. The product purchase is only one part of the arrangement.</p>'],
['Use one current forecast','<p>Track these amounts separately: original contract, approved additions and credits, pending decisions, owner purchases, professional fees and remaining reserve. Record payments as a separate cash-flow column; money paid to date is not the same as final project cost.</p><p>A useful selection log includes the item, allowance basis, selected price, related labor difference, approval date and order deadline. Updating that log before an order is placed gives you a chance to choose a less expensive option.</p>'],
['Approve the change before the changed work',`<p>CSLB’s home-improvement contract guidance calls for written change orders signed before changes to the work. Have the document identify the scope and price adjustment and explain the schedule effect. A verbal selection conversation is not a complete change record. ${ref('contract')}.</p><p>If a price is still unknown, agree on the investigation or next limited step rather than approving an open-ended promise that everything will be settled later.</p>`]
],faq:[['Is an allowance an extra charge on top of the contract?','It depends on how the contract is written, but an allowance commonly sits within the stated price. Confirm that explicitly and establish how the final amount is reconciled.'],['Should I choose the bid with the smallest contingency?','A reserve is not a measure of contractor quality. Compare the scope, known selections and unresolved risks. A small reserve does not remove those risks, and a large one does not excuse an incomplete estimate.']],sources:['contract'],links:[['Compare remodeling estimates','../contractor-estimate-comparison-guide.html'],['Change orders','../change-orders-guide.html'],['Budget planner','../remodel-budget-planner.html']]
},
{
slug:'whole-home-closeout',title:'Remodel Closeout Checklist: Punch List, Permits & Warranties',
description:'Finish a remodel with a clear punch list, permit records, cleaning scope, product manuals, warranty contacts and a documented final walkthrough.',
intro:'The last walkthrough should leave you with more than a clean room and a final invoice. Use it to identify unfinished items, understand the new equipment and collect the records needed to maintain the home. Keep construction completion, permit approval and contractual payment obligations distinct.',
sections:[
['Walk the project with a written punch list','<p>Inspect each room with the contractor and record incomplete or corrective work using a location, photo, agreed action and responsible person. “Kitchen drawer beside the range rubs when opened” is more actionable than “fix cabinets.” Set a follow-up date for each open item.</p><p>Separate corrections from new requests. If you decide at the walkthrough that you want another shelf, document it as a proposed addition rather than silently adding it to the completion list. Keep a record when each agreed item is resolved.</p>'],
['Confirm the permit record',`<p>Ask for the required final inspection status and any outstanding conditions. The City of San Diego explains that final inspection follows dependent approvals in its ${ref('inspections')}. Check the actual project record; a contractor’s final invoice does not establish permit closure.</p><p>Retain approved plans, documented changes and relevant test records. If the scope did not require a permit, keep the project’s written scope and any authority clarification that supported that conclusion.</p>`],
['Define the cleaning and handover scope','<p>Review the agreed cleaning work: removal of debris and protection, cabinet interiors, glass, surfaces and remaining construction dust. Identify specialty cleaning separately rather than assuming the ordinary closeout clean includes every surface or existing area of the home.</p><p>Ask for a demonstration of new controls, shutoffs, ventilation equipment and service access. Have the installer identify which panels or components need to remain reachable. Record model numbers while they are easy to find.</p>'],
['Collect warranties and maintenance instructions',`<p>Build one folder containing contracts, invoices, approved changes, product manuals, warranty terms, serial numbers and service contacts. CSLB advises homeowners to obtain offered warranties in writing. ${ref('contract')}.</p><p>Distinguish the installer’s obligations from the manufacturer’s coverage. Ask where a claim starts, what records are needed and which maintenance instructions apply. Save paint formulas, finish selections and any spare material information so later repairs do not rely on guessing.</p>`],
['Reconcile the final account and follow-up work',`<p>Compare the final statement with the contract, approved changes, allowance adjustments and payments already made. CSLB’s ${ref('checklist')} advises documenting payments and avoiding final payment until satisfied with the work. Review the actual agreement and seek appropriate advice if there is a dispute; a checklist does not decide contractual payment rights.</p><p>Schedule a later maintenance review based on the products and agreed service arrangements. Check operation, sealants and emerging issues, and report concerns promptly under the applicable terms. A one-year calendar reminder is useful organization, not a promise that every component has a one-year warranty.</p>`]
],faq:[['Is the punch list the same as the final inspection?','No. The punch list records agreed construction corrections. The permitting authority’s inspection addresses the permitted work. Keep evidence of both where applicable.'],['What if a warranty issue appears after the final payment?','Use the written warranty and contract to identify the responsible contact and reporting process. Document the condition and date. Payment does not itself tell you the duration or scope of any warranty.']],sources:['inspections','contract','checklist'],links:[['Permit review through final approval','plan-review.html'],['Allowance reconciliation','allowances.html'],['Whole-home project planning','../whole-home-remodel-guide.html']]
}
];
